WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.500
What happens when you want to look at a dynamic value?

00:03.500 --> 00:06.780
In other words, when you can't predict the route that you're going to.

00:06.780 --> 00:09.860
So take for instance, when we go into Pokédex,

00:09.860 --> 00:12.540
and Pokédex has different Pokemon,

00:12.540 --> 00:14.900
that is the way the Pokédex works.

00:14.900 --> 00:17.580
What if we wanted to actually render out the different Pokemon,

00:17.580 --> 00:20.220
but we wanted to do things based on the parameter in the URL?

00:20.220 --> 00:22.260
So an example of this might be,

00:22.260 --> 00:26.700
if we're looking at the Pokédex page and you say,

00:26.700 --> 00:29.620
okay, theoretically what I'd like to be able to do is also then

00:29.620 --> 00:32.980
go to a page that goes to like,

00:32.980 --> 00:36.200
go to slash Pokédex slash one,

00:36.200 --> 00:37.780
and that should call the first Pokemon.

00:37.780 --> 00:40.940
In other words, this part is dynamic because what we don't want to do is define

00:40.940 --> 00:43.500
one dot view, two dot view, and etc.

00:43.500 --> 00:45.780
So how might that actually work?

00:45.780 --> 00:47.540
Well, when it comes to that,

00:47.540 --> 00:51.780
what we're actually looking at is around the idea of dynamic param IDs.

00:51.780 --> 00:56.420
So the way this works is we go inside of our router,

00:56.420 --> 00:58.820
inside of Playground.

00:58.900 --> 01:02.940
What we have here is we have a path here,

01:02.940 --> 01:08.020
that's slash Pokédex slash with the colon ID,

01:08.020 --> 01:10.860
and this is what tells it that it's going to be dynamic.

01:10.860 --> 01:13.340
Then from here, we can then say,

01:13.340 --> 01:15.920
let's create a Pokemon page because this would fetch

01:15.920 --> 01:18.220
a specific Pokemon in this particular case.

01:18.220 --> 01:21.980
Let me FSC this and then let's just say Pokemon.

01:21.980 --> 01:25.220
For now, we'll just say number 100 to show that it's

01:25.220 --> 01:27.460
wrong as far as the URL is concerned.

01:27.460 --> 01:30.060
Then we'll just do component and this time we'll just

01:30.060 --> 01:36.220
import the path at views slash Pokemon page dot view. Save.

01:36.220 --> 01:38.980
So to prove that this works,

01:38.980 --> 01:43.100
we're going to go ahead and on the Pokédex page right here,

01:43.100 --> 01:46.700
we will have a router link that goes to slash

01:46.700 --> 01:48.620
Pokédex and this time we'll do slash 20,

01:48.620 --> 01:49.900
just to show something different.

01:49.900 --> 01:52.380
Go to Pokemon number 20.

01:52.380 --> 01:55.080
We see that here, and if we click on this,

01:55.080 --> 01:58.920
you'll see that it does correctly jump us over to the page.

01:58.920 --> 02:00.280
But what we need is we need to be able to

02:00.280 --> 02:01.880
pull in the dynamic data.

02:01.880 --> 02:04.320
Right? How does that work?

02:04.320 --> 02:07.720
Well, when you used to do it in View2,

02:07.720 --> 02:09.680
the way you would do it in Options API and you could still

02:09.680 --> 02:11.960
could is you do this dot route dot

02:11.960 --> 02:14.680
params that's usually what you would reach out for.

02:14.680 --> 02:16.500
But in an effort to get you all practicing with

02:16.500 --> 02:17.600
composition API and seeing what

02:17.600 --> 02:19.800
an alternative form might look like,

02:19.800 --> 02:21.840
what might you reach for?

02:21.840 --> 02:23.360
Probably a helper method, right?

02:23.360 --> 02:28.040
So we have something called useRoute because it's from ViewRouter.

02:28.040 --> 02:31.720
Then we can say constRoute equals useRoute.

02:31.720 --> 02:33.040
Then before we do anything,

02:33.040 --> 02:35.800
let's just log that out real quick and show you what we get.

02:35.800 --> 02:38.080
Oh, this is the Pokédex page, I need to go back.

02:38.080 --> 02:39.480
So on the Pokédex page,

02:39.480 --> 02:42.360
you'll see now that we expand this out to the target,

02:42.360 --> 02:43.880
it has a bunch of stuff actually,

02:43.880 --> 02:45.880
params, name, meta,

02:45.880 --> 02:47.920
and then if you broke it down to query stuff,

02:47.920 --> 02:48.960
there's a whole ton of stuff

02:48.960 --> 02:50.400
under there that you can unpack.

02:50.400 --> 02:53.560
But the one we care about is going to be the parameters.

02:53.560 --> 02:56.000
So let me go ahead and actually move this useRoute because

02:56.000 --> 02:58.360
where we need it is not the Pokédex page,

02:58.360 --> 03:00.800
we need it on the Pokemon page.

03:00.800 --> 03:02.880
Let me drop this over here instead.

03:02.880 --> 03:05.440
So now let's go ahead and go to Pokemon 20,

03:05.440 --> 03:08.080
and now we'll notice actually we refresh.

03:08.080 --> 03:12.160
There we go. We have our route being rendered,

03:12.160 --> 03:13.760
and we look at our params,

03:13.760 --> 03:17.520
you'll see that we have an ID of 20 in here.

03:17.520 --> 03:19.120
Where does this ID come from?

03:19.120 --> 03:24.240
This ID comes from what we named right here inside of our path.

03:24.240 --> 03:26.320
That's where it's actually being defined.

03:26.320 --> 03:28.640
So what we get if we want,

03:28.640 --> 03:32.080
we say route.params.id,

03:32.080 --> 03:34.680
and we do that and refresh the page,

03:34.680 --> 03:37.160
you'll see there it is, 20.

03:37.160 --> 03:38.440
That's all it takes.

03:38.440 --> 03:42.360
So now your imagination can go from here,

03:42.360 --> 03:43.920
you go to the Pokemon API,

03:43.920 --> 03:45.160
and then when you load the page,

03:45.160 --> 03:46.960
you can go ahead and fetch the right Pokemon,

03:47.160 --> 03:49.240
get its attributes, render it to the page,

03:49.240 --> 03:51.560
but for the sake of conciseness,

03:51.560 --> 03:54.080
we're just going to go ahead and show that here,

03:54.080 --> 03:56.920
just like this, route.params.id,

03:56.920 --> 03:59.840
and then just like that,

03:59.840 --> 04:02.720
you now have a dynamic page that can take any argument,

04:02.720 --> 04:04.320
and then however you see fit,

04:04.320 --> 04:07.120
you can programmatically set up else conditions,

04:07.120 --> 04:10.920
if else whatever, not to render the right stuff to your users.

04:10.920 --> 04:14.280
What about params for parent routes?

04:14.280 --> 04:16.640
Okay, so the question here is around params for parent routes.

04:16.640 --> 04:19.720
So what you're saying is that if it's like with,

04:19.720 --> 04:21.480
so basically let's sketch this out real quick,

04:21.480 --> 04:22.680
are you saying something like,

04:22.680 --> 04:26.320
if Pokedex had then like on top of ID for the Pokemon,

04:26.320 --> 04:29.200
there was like an additional thing for like element.

04:29.200 --> 04:30.680
So then you might be on like an element page,

04:30.680 --> 04:32.720
and you want to look at your ID params.

04:32.720 --> 04:33.560
Potentially.

04:33.560 --> 04:37.760
Yeah, so I believe the moment you do the route basically,

04:38.760 --> 04:39.840
you should basically get everything

04:39.840 --> 04:41.720
that comes as a part of that route.

04:41.720 --> 04:44.600
And so this goes beyond the scope

04:44.600 --> 04:46.440
of like the fundamentals review router,

04:46.440 --> 04:48.040
but there's even things like nested views

04:48.040 --> 04:49.640
and all those things that we can play around with.

04:49.640 --> 04:51.080
So you should be able to get the parent

04:51.080 --> 04:52.880
from that path without any problem.

04:52.880 --> 04:55.400
And that's why it would be a good practice, for example,

04:55.400 --> 04:59.200
not in your router.js to say,

04:59.200 --> 05:03.960
to define another route that uses the same parameter like name.

05:03.960 --> 05:05.800
Like you wouldn't want to be like ID ID.

05:05.800 --> 05:07.600
Cause I think that would actually probably,

05:07.600 --> 05:09.880
I'm almost certain that would break view router,

05:09.880 --> 05:11.080
cause it would be confused.

05:11.080 --> 05:12.040
And so in that particular case,

05:12.040 --> 05:14.240
that's where it's important to be just specific

05:14.240 --> 05:15.840
about what that parameter name is,

05:15.840 --> 05:18.160
and then the route should contain all of those parameters

05:18.160 --> 05:20.860
correctly appended into that object.

05:22.760 --> 05:26.960
So you said nested routes, could you have like region

05:26.960 --> 05:28.280
was the thing you were saying?

05:28.280 --> 05:29.120
Yeah.

05:29.120 --> 05:31.800
Region slash region ID, a region name.

05:31.800 --> 05:32.640
Yep.

05:32.640 --> 05:35.360
Underneath that have the pokedex for that region

05:35.360 --> 05:37.720
and then have a poked Pokemon page.

05:37.720 --> 05:38.560
Yeah.

05:38.560 --> 05:39.720
The ID parameter on that.

05:39.720 --> 05:40.560
We can.

05:40.560 --> 05:42.280
And so it's not part of this exercise,

05:42.280 --> 05:43.400
but since this is a question,

05:43.400 --> 05:45.240
I do want to show this for the nested.

05:45.240 --> 05:47.040
If we look at the docs right here,

05:47.040 --> 05:48.840
and we look at nested routes,

05:49.880 --> 05:51.960
the thing about nested routes is it's easy to think of it

05:51.960 --> 05:53.000
like what we just did,

05:53.000 --> 05:55.280
cause you see here a user Johnny profile, right?

05:55.280 --> 05:58.400
You're like, didn't we just do this pokedex slash Pokemon?

05:58.400 --> 06:01.220
This is different because a nested view means

06:01.220 --> 06:03.920
that you have a router view inside of a router view,

06:03.920 --> 06:06.040
meaning the rest of the page stays the same

06:06.040 --> 06:08.920
and just that one section of the page changes

06:08.920 --> 06:10.640
based on the URL.

06:10.640 --> 06:12.800
So as you can see here inside of this example,

06:12.800 --> 06:14.080
let me blow it up a lot,

06:14.080 --> 06:15.280
is that the user page out here,

06:15.280 --> 06:17.520
which is the shell, which is slash user,

06:18.600 --> 06:21.600
is currently bringing in the Johnny child, right?

06:21.600 --> 06:23.240
So it'd be user Johnny.

06:23.240 --> 06:27.320
But the nested view here is for the profile and the posts.

06:27.320 --> 06:29.480
And so you can almost think of it like tabs

06:29.480 --> 06:31.360
where you don't want the whole page to re-render

06:31.360 --> 06:33.660
if you click on post for Johnny.

06:33.660 --> 06:34.720
You want everything else to stay the same,

06:34.720 --> 06:37.000
you just want to switch the tab that's inside of it.

06:37.000 --> 06:39.620
And so nested views are basically this concept

06:39.620 --> 06:42.200
that without having to re-render the entire page,

06:42.200 --> 06:43.840
you can just render a portion of it

06:43.840 --> 06:46.080
and have that update based on the URL.

06:46.080 --> 06:48.360
And so that can be highly performant as well

06:48.360 --> 06:49.600
when it comes to doing things for your app.

06:49.600 --> 06:53.280
So I know it's funny that nested views have had a resurgence

06:53.280 --> 06:54.440
lately in the discussion,

06:54.440 --> 06:56.280
but this has been in view for quite a while.

06:56.280 --> 06:57.880
But I do want to make you all aware of it,

06:57.880 --> 06:59.240
so that if you ever find that you're like,

06:59.240 --> 07:01.040
oh, everything else needs to change,

07:01.040 --> 07:02.640
or sorry, everything needs to stay the same,

07:02.640 --> 07:04.720
except for this one part when the URL swaps,

07:04.720 --> 07:07.580
nested views are nested routes, check this out,

07:07.580 --> 07:09.080
this will solve your problems.

